\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.2}{\ignorespaces Repair pattern for rule~5: one exclusive choice across two parts of the net. The first choice leaves a token that records the decision (dashed places and arcs). \texttt  {LL\_delivery} can only fire if \texttt  {LL\_mixed} was skipped. The skip of the second choice exists twice, once for each token, so no control token remains at the end. \texttt  {LL\_receive} is connected like \texttt  {LL\_delivery}, and HO2 is repaired in the same way. Per handling area, the pattern adds two places and one silent transition.}}{58}{figure.caption.35}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:mc_repair_xor}{{5.2}{58}{Repair pattern for rule~5: one exclusive choice across two parts of the net. The first choice leaves a token that records the decision (dashed places and arcs). \texttt {LL\_delivery} can only fire if \texttt {LL\_mixed} was skipped. The skip of the second choice exists twice, once for each token, so no control token remains at the end. \texttt {LL\_receive} is connected like \texttt {LL\_delivery}, and HO2 is repaired in the same way. Per handling area, the pattern adds two places and one silent transition}{figure.caption.35}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.3}{\ignorespaces Marker logic for rule~3 (at least one stop), shown for two optional stops in parallel. Gate In puts a token into \emph  {no stop yet}. Each stop exists twice: the first stop moves the token to \emph  {stop done}, a further stop only reads it (double arrow). Gate Out needs \emph  {stop done}, so a visit cannot leave without a stop. If both stops are skipped, however, the token stays in \emph  {no stop yet} and the visit can never finish. The complete construction therefore also forbids skipping the last open stop, with one more control place per stop (not shown).}}{58}{figure.caption.36}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:mc_repair_stop}{{5.3}{58}{Marker logic for rule~3 (at least one stop), shown for two optional stops in parallel. Gate In puts a token into \emph {no stop yet}. Each stop exists twice: the first stop moves the token to \emph {stop done}, a further stop only reads it (double arrow). Gate Out needs \emph {stop done}, so a visit cannot leave without a stop. If both stops are skipped, however, the token stays in \emph {no stop yet} and the visit can never finish. The complete construction therefore also forbids skipping the last open stop, with one more control place per stop (not shown)}{figure.caption.36}{}}
